                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_board:         `<strong>That's the right anchor.</strong> The board asked for a 12% margin — the aspiration is reaching it, which requires securing the SiC revenue. Now Path A's 15% chance of a −€70M write-off can be judged against a real line, not in the abstract.`,
                    coach_r1_breakeven:     `<strong>Defensible, but thin.</strong> Break-even on capital is a reference point — but the board's actual target is the 12% margin. An aspiration that ignores why the OEM revenue matters under-weights what's truly at stake.`,
                    coach_r1_maxev:         `<strong>That's not an aspiration — it's a tautology.</strong> “Whatever the highest-EV path delivers” lets the EV calculation set its own reference. The board didn't ask for the highest average; it asked for a 12% margin. Draw the line independently.`,
                    coach_r1_avoidloss:     `<strong>Loss-aversion is steering you.</strong> “Avoid any negative NPV” makes Path A's 15% write-off look fatal and pushes you toward walking away — even though walking away cedes 31% of revenue. A reference point that only fears losses can't weigh a strategic upside.`,
                    coach_r1_noev:          `<strong>Re-check your EV.</strong> Path A has the highest expected value here — its big +€130M upside dominates the average. If you ranked another path on top, you haven't actually computed the probability-weighted payoff.`,
                    coach_r2_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp regret analysis.</strong> Path B minimizes anticipated regret, Path A carries the largest maximum regret, and only Path A's worst state breaches 3.0x. You've made the EV-vs-regret tension explicit — exactly what the committee needs to decide out loud.`,
                    coach_r2_partial:       `<strong>Partly there.</strong> Some of your regret-matrix reads are right, but check the others. Min-regret and max-EV pointing to different paths is the whole lesson — get the matrix exact before you lean on it.`,
                    coach_r2_wrong:         `<strong>Rebuild the matrix.</strong> Regret in each state is the best-achievable payoff minus the one you'd receive. Path B's high on-time odds make it the low-regret choice; Path A's −€70M tail is the high-regret one. The numbers, not the narrative, decide.`,
                    coach_r2_covenant:      `<strong>You've under-sized the covenant risk.</strong> Only Path A's worst state — the −€70M write-off — pushes net-debt/EBITDA past 3.0x. That tail isn't just a bad outcome; it trips a covenant. Risk exposure has to be sized against the constraint, not just the payoff.`,
                    coach_r2_deliberate:    `<strong>A deliberate criterion.</strong> You read the matrix correctly AND took a clear stance on whether this committee optimizes for the average or for not being the people who made the avoidable mistake. That's a decision, not a flinch.`,
                    coach_r3_holdA:         `<strong>Holding A — eyes open.</strong> You re-priced at 45% and held with a pre-set trigger. That's an updated decision, not inertia: the big upside still clears your aspiration. Just keep the covenant tail in view.`,
                    coach_r3_inertiaA:      `<strong>Is this conviction or inertia?</strong> You held Path A after the yield news with no pre-set trigger. The €22M and eighteen months are pulling on the room. Recompute as if the program started today at 45% — does it still win?`,
                    coach_r3_switchB:       `<strong>A clean switch.</strong> The signal pushed Path A's odds below your pre-set threshold, so you moved to the lower-regret, covenant-safe licensing path. New information moved you — not anxiety.`,
                    coach_r3_flinchB:       `<strong>The right path, the wrong reason.</strong> Switching to B is reasonable on the merits — but without a pre-set trigger, was it the 45% number or the anxiety that moved you? Decision consistency means you'd have known your threshold in advance.`,
                    coach_r3_flinch:        `<strong>You reacted to the most recent number.</strong> Switching the instant the leak dropped, with no pre-committed threshold, is flinching, not updating. If you'd set the trigger in Round 3, you'd know whether 45% was actually your switch point.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring the signal is its own error.</strong> A leaked yield result that re-prices Path A from 55% to 45% is exactly the information a committee should fold in. Holding the Round-1 plan unchanged isn't discipline — it's refusing to update.`,
                    coach_r3_hedge:         `<strong>A hedge that breaks the bank.</strong> €75M against a €90M envelope leaves €15M for everything else — and the worst-case write-off still pushes you to 3.1x, breaching the 3.0x covenant. A hedge that triggers a covenant breach isn't a hedge; it's two bets you can't both afford. Re-size it or pick one.`,
                    coach_r3_walk:          `<strong>Safe, but you ceded the platform.</strong> Walking away is certain and covenant-safe — but the OEM almost certainly dual-sources, putting 31% of revenue at structural risk and making the 12% margin unreachable. Was that consistent with your Round-1 aspiration?`,
                    coach_r3_deadline:      `<strong>Plan for the deadline you have.</strong> The OEM's RFP is binding and a competitor has already bid — assuming an extension is wishful. If you genuinely need more time, that's a reason to favour the faster-qualifying path, not to ignore the clock.`,
                    coach_r4_sunk:          `<strong>Sunk cost is steering the €60M.</strong> “Eighteen months shouldn't be wasted” — but that €22M is gone whether you spend the next euro or not. Recompute the decision as if the in-house program started today at zero. If your answer changes, you just found sunk cost driving the bet.`,
                    coach_r4_push:          `<strong>Good money after bad.</strong> A €20M “one more push” on a path that has already turned is the textbook escalation of commitment. The forward odds, not the desire to vindicate the earlier choice, should decide whether more capital goes in.`,
                    coach_r4_cut:           `<strong>You cut losses cleanly.</strong> Stopping capital into a turned bet — independent of how much is already spent — is the hygienic move. The board chair will respect a committee that can stop.`,
                    coach_r4_renegotiate:   `<strong>Sensible on Path B.</strong> A licensing concern invites renegotiation, not abandonment — the module qualified on time. Holding while you renegotiate protects the position without throwing capital at a recovery.`,
                    coach_r4_trail:         `<strong>That's the defence the chair wants.</strong> A documented trail — aspiration, EV, regret, covenant — lets you defend the decision on its process, separate from how the dice landed. Decision quality is judged before the outcome is known.`,
                    coach_r4_outcome:       `<strong>That's outcome bias.</strong> “It turned out well, so it was right” can't survive a board chair who knows luck from judgement. A bad process that got lucky is still a bad process — defend the trail, not the result.`,
                    coach_r4_champion:      `<strong>Conviction isn't a defence.</strong> Leaning on the R&D head's belief that the team will deliver is exactly the escalation pressure the room must resist. The board chair asked for the analysis, not the champion's faith.`,

